"A thing with nothing at stake does not think.": "98bc86199a64",
"Buddhist thought divides the question at a different joint. What makes something a sentient being, a sattva, is the capacity to feel, to be pleased and pained, and the tradition does not rank beings by how cleverly they reason. A god who reasons superbly and an insect that cannot reason at all are both within the scope of compassion, because both can suffer. So the Buddhist question about a machine is the one this lesson put third. Whether it calculates is plain, and whether it understands is of secondary interest. Whether there is feeling there decides everything, including whether the first precept applies to it.": "d14a28a2b01a",
"For seventy years the debate concerned imaginary machines. It now concerns real ones. Systems trained on enormous quantities of human writing carry on conversations that most people cannot tell from a persons, and they were built by neither of the methods the philosophers had in mind. Nobody wrote their rule book. They adjust billions of numerical settings until they predict text well, and what goes on inside them is only partly understood by the people who make them. One camp sees the Chinese room come true: a system that has learned which strings of words follow which, with no access to the world the words are about. Another replies that to predict what a physicist will say next one must model a good deal of physics, and that understanding may come in degrees and consist in such abilities. Lovelaces limit is equally unsettled, since these systems produce things nobody ordered them to produce.": "f4877e82764b",
"He then declined to consider it.": "a5da3eebad1a",
"He would have had the maker executed on the spot.": "23087b256543",
"In 1950 the English mathematician Alan Turing opened a paper with the sentence I propose to consider the question, Can machines think? He then declined to consider it. The words machine and think, he said, are too vague for the question to be settled by argument, and he replaced it with a test. An interrogator exchanges typed messages with two hidden parties, a human being and a machine, and must decide which is which. If the machine is taken for the human about as often as a human would be, it has passed. Turing called this the imitation game. He expected machines to play it well within fifty years, and he made a second prediction that has attracted less notice: at the end of the century the use of words and general educated opinion will have altered so much that one will be able to speak of machines thinking without expecting to be contradicted.": "3b284fa081a3",
"In 1980 the American philosopher John Searle turned the mill into a room and put himself inside it. He imagined a program that passes the Turing test in Chinese, a language of which he knew not a word. He is locked in a room with baskets of Chinese characters and a rule book in English. Slips of paper covered in characters are pushed under the door. He looks up their shapes in the book, which tells him which characters to push back. To the people outside, the replies are those of a fluent speaker. Searle has done everything the computer does: he has run the program. And he understands nothing. He does not know whether the slips are about the weather or about his mother. His conclusion is that carrying out a program, however good, is not sufficient for understanding. A program is defined by its syntax, the rules for moving symbols according to their shapes. Understanding requires semantics, knowing what the symbols mean, and no amount of shuffling will supply it.": "07cad04134c8",
"It proved to be an assembly of leather, wood, glue, and lacquer.": "e5dbe8fad226",
"Leibniz gave the second reaction the form of an argument, and he gave it against his own machines. Suppose, he wrote in 1714, that there were a machine so constructed as to think, feel, and have perception. Imagine it enlarged until one could walk into it as into a mill. Inside we should, on examining its interior, find only parts which work one upon another, and never anything by which to explain a perception. A century later Ada Lovelace, describing the Analytical Engine that Charles Babbage had designed, added a different limit. The engine, she wrote, has no pretensions whatever to originate anything. It can do whatever we know how to order it to perform. Leibnizs objection concerns experience and Lovelaces concerns originality. They have been confused with each other ever since.": "10f70e48eebc",
"Moreover, it must be confessed that perception and that which depends upon it are inexplicable on mechanical grounds, that is to say, by means of figures and motions. And supposing there were a machine, so constructed as to think, feel, and have perception, it might be conceived as increased in size, while keeping the same proportions, so that one might go into it as into a mill. That being so, we should, on examining its interior, find only parts which work one upon another, and never anything by which to explain a perception.": "182d3b23c14b",
"No amount of shuffling will supply it.": "d7ea5635f0d8",
"On what feeling requires, the tradition gives two hints that point in different directions. The Abhidharma analyzes a mind into momentary events defined by what they do and what they condition, with no owner behind them, which is closer to functionalism than anything in ancient Western thought. Yet the early discourses also insist that consciousness never occurs alone. It depends on name-and-form, the living body with its feeling and perception, and that in turn depends on consciousness: the two stand, says Sāriputta, like two sheaves of reeds leaning up against each other, and if one is pulled away the other falls. And a being, in the traditional account, arrives in a new life carrying a stream of consciousness from a previous one. A device that is assembled and switched on has no place in that account, and the texts, composed long before such devices, are silent. Buddhist teachers asked about it today give different answers (Dharma and the West, D3 and D12).": "6b66cec96ae8",
"One camp sees the Chinese room come true.": "e4889b2a893d",
"Searle chose Chinese because he could not read it. A reader who can should substitute a language that is closed to him, and the exercise below does so.": "70b208f144b4",
"The engine has no pretensions whatever to originate anything.": "43d9c23b808a",
"The idea behind the test was three centuries old. Reason, wrote Thomas Hobbes in 1651, is nothing but Reckoning (that is, Adding and Substracting) of the consequences of names. If thinking is calculation, then anything that calculates thinks, and what it is made of is irrelevant. Leibniz, who built one of the first machines that could multiply, dreamed of a notation in which every dispute could be settled as accountants settle theirs: Let us calculate. The modern version of the idea is called functionalism. A mental state, it says, is defined by what it does, by its causes and effects within a system, in the way that a program is defined by its steps and can run on machines of quite different construction. On this view the mind stands to the brain as software stands to hardware, and a suitably programmed computer would not merely imitate thinking. It would think.": "e53a47d5171f",
"The machine is taken for the human.": "711c0e967165",
"The opposite intuition is as old. The Daoist book Liezi tells how an artificer named Yan Shi presented King Mu of Zhou with a performer he had made. It walked, bowed, and looked about, truly a human being. When its chin was touched it sang in tune, and when its hand was lifted it danced in time. The king took it for a real person, until at the end of the show it winked at his concubines. He was furious and would have had the maker executed on the spot. Yan Shi at once took the figure apart, and it proved to be an assembly of leather, wood, glue, and lacquer, colored white, black, red, and blue. Every organ was there. The king removed the heart, and the mouth could not speak. He removed the liver, and the eyes could not see. Delighted, he asked: Can human skill really equal the work of the Maker of things? The story records the two reactions people still have. While the performance lasts we see a person, and when the case is opened we see parts.": "c12840af696c",
"The paper drew more replies than almost any other in the field. The systems reply grants that the man does not understand and says that he is only one component: the understanding belongs to the whole system of man, book, and baskets, as it belongs to a brain and to no single neuron. Searle answered that he could memorize the book and do the work in his head, and he would still understand nothing. The robot reply says that the symbols mean nothing because they are connected to nothing: give the system cameras and limbs, so that its word for tea is caused by tea, and meaning will follow. Searle answered that the cameras signals would reach him as more symbols. The brain simulator reply imagines a program that copies, neuron by neuron, the brain of a Chinese speaker. Searle answered that a simulation of a storm leaves everyone dry. Each exchange ends in a clash of intuitions about the same point, and no decisive move has been found by either side.": "c361d3452e88",
"The question he set aside in 1950.": "f59cdba7d6dc",
"Turings second prediction, at least, has come true. People speak of machines thinking, and few bother to contradict them. Whether the usage records a discovery or only a habit is the question he set aside in 1950. It helps to keep three things apart. A machine may match us at tasks, and that can be measured. It may or may not understand, and here the room is still the best test of what we mean by the word. And it may or may not feel, which was the subject of the last lesson. We can only see a short distance ahead, Turing ended his paper, but we can see plenty there that needs to be done.": "70b46f3426e5",
